From 81dd9e4075a7a8e2afe4db4ec0ab449ba2809202 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: niri patch <niri@local>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 15:11:28 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] render: apply gamma ramps in software when there's no
 hardware LUT

The Apple DCP (Apple Silicon, Asahi Linux) exposes only CTM on its CRTC and
no GAMMA_LUT, and a 3x3 matrix cannot express the three 1D ramps of
zwlr_gamma_control_v1 (see wlroots#1078). Gamma control on such hardware
fails for every client, so wlsunset, gammastep, DankMaterialShell's night
mode and friends silently do nothing.

Fall back to applying the ramp while rendering, like the software gamma
fallback wlroots grew in !5166: advertise a 256 entry ramp - one entry per
8 bit framebuffer level, so nothing is lost compared to a hardware LUT of
the same depth - and hand it to the renderer instead of the CRTC.

The ramp is applied by an element on top of the output that copies the
freshly composited pixels into a texture and draws them back through a
shader looking every channel up in the ramp, which is uploaded as a 256x1
texture on unit 1. A ramp is a per-pixel function, so this is done for
exactly the damage rectangles handed to draw(), and costs proportionally to
the damaged area. Doing it per rectangle is also what keeps it correct:
pixels outside the damage still hold the transformed values from an earlier
frame, and transforming them again would compound. For the same reason all
the copying happens before anything is drawn back - damage rectangles may
overlap, and the transform must not see its own output.

Note that this deliberately does not use is_framebuffer_effect(): that
machinery is built for blur, where a changed pixel affects a whole
neighborhood, so it re-captures and repaints the entire element whenever
anything below it changes. For a point-wise transform that would turn every
one pixel of damage into a full output recomposite - measured at +0.5 W on
a 3024x1964 panel with a 20 Hz clock as the only damage source.

What is still lost while a ramp is set is direct scanout, since the frame
has to go through the renderer. An identity ramp is treated as no ramp at
all, which is what clients leave behind when night light is scheduled but
inactive, so nothing is drawn and nothing is paid for in that case.

Since this needs no DRM at all, it also gives the winit backend working
gamma control. `debug { force-software-gamma }` takes the software path even
when the hardware has a LUT, for testing.
